    "body": "<!-- claude-projects-attribution: {\"github_login\":\"siainbuletin\"} -->\n_Requested by **Sia** · [project thread](https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2YZxiADvTCtDjAA91N2hKJ7)_\n\n## What\n\n`org-table-widget` was already enabled in `sp-textui`, but it ran with defaults that look nothing like Obsidian, and `org-modern` was drawing its own borders over the same tables (its README says to turn `org-modern-table` off). In `sp-org-tables`:\n\n- `org-modern-table` is `nil`; zebra stripes are off.\n- Widget borders and header row take quiet colours blended from the theme (`sp-org-tables-border-strength` 0.22, `sp-org-tables-header-strength` 0.07), recomputed on `enable-theme-functions`.\n- `sp-org-tables-quiet-pipes-mode` (on in Org buffers) dims `|` and rule lines of the plain-text table, for terminals and while a table is revealed for editing.\n\n## Is it possible?\n\nNearly, in a GUI. The widget always draws a full grid (outer box and vertical bars), so borders can be made faint but not removed. For fully borderless Obsidian rows a different renderer would be needed. In plain text, Org cannot hide the pipes without hurting editing, so they are only dimmed.\n\n## Tested\n\n4 ERT tests in Emacs 29.3 batch (colour blend, `org-modern-table`, dimmed pipes). The doc tangles to the module. Not seen in a graphical Emacs: please restart and look at a table, then adjust the two strength options to taste.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01WWLNiApPuquoY1rg2MPhPr)_",
